Answer: Characters can be represented using their ASCII values. For example: A → 65 B → 66 C → 67 D → 68 E → 69 A loop can therefore iterate from 65 to 69:

```java
for (int i = 65; i <= 69; i++) {
System.out.print((char)i);
}
```

The integer value can be converted to a character using: (char)i This allows alphabet patterns to be generated using numeric loops.

Answer: The pattern is: A A B A B C A B C D A B C D E The row number determines how many characters should be printed. One approach is to use ASCII values:

```java
for (int j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
for (int i = 0; i <= j; i++) {
System.out.print((char)('A' + i));
}
System.out.println();
}
```

The character expression: (char)('A' + i) produces: A B C D E depending on the value of i. Another approach is to use an additional character variable and increment it inside the loop.

